   "caveat": "Optimistic by construction: the simulator makes struggling learners slower and more hesitant. This validates the implementation, not real learner behaviour.",
   "verdict": "Supported (in simulation)"
  },
  {
   "id": "H2",
   "claim": "The system distinguishes \"doesn't know it\" from \"knows it but can't say it in English\".",
   "supported": true,
   "evidence": "False \"doesn't know\" labels on concepts the learner knows: 72.1% → 12.8% (McNemar χ² 1498.6, p < 0.001). Cost: true-gap recall 95.7% → 70.8%.",
   "caveat": "Measured against simulator ground truth. Recall of genuine gaps drops; see the trade-off below.",
   "verdict": "Supported (in simulation)"
  },
  {
   "id": "H3",
   "claim": "The diagnosed bottleneck matches the true cause better than chance.",
   "supported": true,
   "evidence": "Accuracy 54.5% (cluster-bootstrap CI 53.1%–56.2%) vs chance 16.7% and majority-class 26.4%; Cohen's κ 0.45; 20.2% deferred as uncertain.",
   "caveat": "Moderate, not strong: retrieval vs overload and knowledge vs Tamil transfer are often confused.",
   "verdict": "Supported (in simulation)"
  },
  {
   "id": "H4",
   "claim": "Adaptive communication increases independent English production and learning.",
   "supported": true,
   "evidence": "Learning gain vs static +13.8 pp (CI +12.6 pp to +15.1 pp, d_z 0.99, p < 0.001); vs time-matched retries +12.2 pp. Independent completion +31.5 pp. Positive at every assumption scale ≥ 0.5.",
   "caveat": "Learning gains depend on modelled learning effects. With zero assumed teaching effect the advantage disappears (see sensitivity), so the size is an assumption.",
   "verdict": "Supported (in simulation)"
  },
  {
   "id": "H5",
   "claim": "Changing the communication channel lowers measured cognitive load on the next attempt.",
   "supported": true,
   "evidence": "Load change after a mode switch −0.166 vs after holding the mode +0.098; difference −0.265 (CI −0.282 to −0.249, p < 0.001).",
   "caveat": "Holding the mode is used as the control because load falls after any high-load answer (regression to the mean). The two situations are not randomised, so this is suggestive rather than causal.",
   "verdict": "Supported (in simulation)"
  },
  {
   "id": "H6",
   "claim": "Each adaptive strategy contributes to learning.",
   "supported": false,
   "partial": true,
